            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00111:alt:fig-00019-alt",
              "text": "Acetylene reacts with sodium amide to produce sodium acetylide that further reacts with an alkyl bromide to give a terminal alkyne."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_09_EC_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00020",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00111:alt:fig-00020-alt",
              "text": "A terminal alkyne reacts with sodium amide to produce sodium acetylide that further reacts with an alkyl bromide to give an internal alkyne."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_09_EC_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00021",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00111:alt:fig-00021-alt",
              "text": "The figure shows an alkyne reacting with a hydrogen halide in ether to give a dihaloalkene. This further reacts with hydrogen halide in ether to form a tetrahaloalkane."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_09_EC_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00022",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00111:alt:fig-00022-alt",
